rries a movable weight. Vertical translation and tilt become coupled when the weight is shifted away from the center. In a calculated frequency sweep, a sensor placed at one corner shows a deep response minimum that is absent from the platform's average motion. The minimum results from cancellation between translation and rotation, rather than from complete isolation of the load. Moving the sensor changes its apparent location. We derive the small-motion equations and verify that symmetry restores decoupling for a centered load. Spring damping is represented phenomenologically, and large-angle motion is excluded. The analysis illustrates why the location of a vibration measurement matters when comparing isolators. A single favorable trace does not capture the motion of the whole platform.","measurement_context":null,"character_measurement":null,"roundtrip_exact":true},{"id":"academic-en-0033","collection":"dataset/academic/en.jsonl","text":"Cache keys for a map tile renderer\nA map renderer is assumed to depend on coordinates, zoom level, style version, and label language. A cache keyed only by coordinates and zoom can return an image that is geometrically correct but linguistically wrong. Adding the language field repairs that collision while leaving style-version collisions unresolved. We enumerate pairs of requests that should share an output and pairs that must remain distinct, using those relations as tests of the key function. The analysis also separates semantic equality from byte equality because compression may vary without changing the rendered content. The principal result is a reproducible method for deriving a cache key from declared dependencies rather than appending fields only after individual stale-output bugs appear.","token_ids":[8397,7039,369,264,2472,10228,20294,198,32,2472,20294,374,19655,311,6904,389,14259,11,15932,2237,11,1742,2373,11,323,2440,4221,13,362,6636,91629,1193,555,14259,323,15932,649,471,459,2217,430,374,53584,750,4495,719,39603,38